WEBVTT

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:05.530
The latch tool is most commonly used
to reverse stitches,

00:00:05.563 --> 00:00:09.370
turning knit stitches into purl stitches.

00:00:09.403 --> 00:00:15.680
Notice how the purl bump is showing
against the knit stitches in this example.

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:21.320
To reform an existing stitch,
push the tool into a stitch below the one

00:00:21.353 --> 00:00:24.530
that's currently in work on a needle.

00:00:24.563 --> 00:00:30.160
Drop the stitch off the needle
and ravel it, forming a ladder.

00:00:30.193 --> 00:00:38.520
Use the tool to pick up each ladder,
forming new stitches.

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:43.410
To create a column of reformed stitches,
drop a single stitch,

00:00:43.443 --> 00:00:48.320
pick up each ladder, forming new stitches.

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:58.210
When the last strand has been knit,
rehang the stitch on the needle.

00:00:58.243 --> 00:01:02.600
Now you have a column
of reformed stitches.

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:12.680
This can be done across a row
for a turning row or a manually

00:01:12.713 --> 00:01:16.520
formed decorative purl row.

00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:22.370
Anytime you use a latch tool, remember,
you're positioning a needle

00:01:22.403 --> 00:01:26.130
in the opposite direction
of the needles on the needle bed.

00:01:26.163 --> 00:01:31.290
If you reform stitches,
they will be facing the opposite way.

00:01:31.323 --> 00:01:34.720
Think about knit and purl
stitches as in hand knitting.

